
  <sn><md>### Copying Specific Files Instead of an Entire Folder

If you want to copy only specific files, you have several options in Rclone.

:::.box .bg-indigo
##### Copy a Single File

Use `rclone copyto`:

```bash
rclone copyto -P ~/Documents/notes.pdf gd:Backup/notes.pdf
```

This copies:

```text
~/Documents/notes.pdf
        ↓
gd:Backup/notes.pdf
```

You can change the destination filename:

```bash
rclone copyto -P ~/Documents/notes.pdf gd:Backup/my-notes.pdf
```
:::


:::.box .bg-purple

##### Copy Multiple Specific Files

Use `rclone copy` and specify files with `--include`:

```bash
rclone copy -P ~/Documents gd:Backup \
  --include "notes.pdf" \
  --include "assignment.pdf"
```

Only these files will be copied:

```text
notes.pdf
assignment.pdf
```
:::


:::.box .bg-teal

##### Copy Files by Extension

For example, copy only PDF files:

```bash
rclone copy -P ~/Documents gd:Backup --include "*.pdf"
```

Copy only images:

```bash
rclone copy -P ~/Documents gd:Backup --include "*.{jpg,png}"
```

A more reliable approach is to use separate include rules:

```bash
rclone copy -P ~/Documents gd:Backup \
  --include "*.jpg" \
  --include "*.png"
```
:::

:::.box .bg-orange

##### Copy Specific Files from Subfolders

Suppose you have:

```text
Documents/
├── notes.txt
├── image.jpg
└── Study/
    ├── chapter1.pdf
    └── chapter2.pdf
```

To copy only `chapter1.pdf`:

```bash
rclone copy -P ~/Documents gd:Backup \
  --include "Study/chapter1.pdf"
```
:::


:::.box .bg-red

##### Use `--files-from`

For a large list of specific files, create a text file:

```text
files.txt
```

Example content:

```text
notes.pdf
assignment.pdf
Study/chapter1.pdf
```

Then run:

```bash
rclone copy -P ~/Documents gd:Backup --files-from files.txt
```
:::


**Quick Reference**

| Task                            | Command                                           |
| ------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |
| Copy one file                   | `rclone copyto -P file remote:path/file`          |
| Copy selected files             | `rclone copy --include "file1" --include "file2"` |
| Copy PDFs only                  | `rclone copy --include "*.pdf"`                   |
| Copy specific files recursively | `rclone copy --include "path/file"`               |
| Copy files from a list          | `rclone copy --files-from files.txt`              |

For copying **one specific file**, `rclone copyto` is usually the simplest and clearest option.
</md></sn>
